    "Necessary in a democratic society": "The interference cannot be deemed necessary in a democratic society. First, while there may be a pressing social need to protect the reputation of prison staff and prevent disorder, the applicant’s statements concerned allegations of torture, a matter of significant public interest that warrants strong protection under Article 10 (see paragraphs 12-14). Second, disbarment is not the least restrictive measure available; lesser sanctions could have addressed any legitimate concerns. Third, the domestic authorities did not provide relevant and sufficient reasons to justify such a severe restriction. The applicant, as a lawyer, was acting in his client’s defence with a reasonable basis for his statements, having observed injuries and heard his client’s account (see paragraph 12), and he promptly lodged official complaints with the authorities (see paragraph 15). The ECtHR has emphasized that disbarment requires compelling reasons (see no. 81024/12) and that lawyers must be able to speak out about serious human rights violations (see no. 29369/10). The speed of the proceedings (see paragraph 17) and the context of systematic targeting of human rights lawyers in Azerbaijan (see paragraphs 29-30) further undermine the necessity of the interference."
